83	<div class="page">
84	<header class="masthead">
85	<p class="eyebrow">Fortress revival &middot; 2026-08-19 to 10-08</p>
86	<h1>The revival so far</h1>
87	<p class="standfirst">Written 2026-10-08 for the curator. Dates are 2026, times UTC. Each part ends with its sources.</p>
88	</header>
89	
90	<nav class="parts" aria-label="Parts">
91	<h2>Six parts</h2>
92	<ol>
93	<li><a href="#part1">The start and the exploring era</a></li>
94	<li><a href="#part2">The unsealed tree and the batches</a></li>
95	<li><a href="#part3">How the record grew</a></li>
96	<li><a href="#part4">Since 10-04: the skills and the process</a></li>
97	<li><a href="#part5">What the revival changed in Fortress</a></li>
98	<li><a href="#part6">The original Fortress and the revival's resolutions</a></li>
99	
100	</ol>
101	</nav>
102	
103	<div class="intro">
104	<p>Words used throughout:</p>
105	<dl class="gloss">
106	<div><dt>team</dt><dd>Sun Labs designers of Fortress. Their last commit is <code>a874948ac</code> (2012-08-31).</dd></div>
107	<div><dt>walk</dt><dd>Interpreter. It runs a program with no static types.</dd></div>
108	<div><dt>compiled path</dt><dd>Path that compiles a program to JVM bytecode and runs it: <code>fortress compile</code>, then <code>fortress run</code>.</dd></div>
109	<div><dt>checker</dt><dd>Static type checker. It runs on the compiled path only.</dd></div>
110	<div><dt>library</dt><dd>Fortress code that every program loads. There were two. The interpreter's is <strong>the one library</strong>. The compiler's own smaller one is <strong>the prelude</strong>.</dd></div>
111	<div><dt>switch-over</dt><dd>Point at which the compiled path moves onto the one library and the prelude is deleted. It comes when the checker accepts the one library.</dd></div>
112	<div><dt>gap ledger</dt><dd>File with one numbered row for each known gap or defect.</dd></div>
113	</dl>
114	</div>
115	
116	<section id="part1" class="part">
117	<h2><span class="num">1</span>The start and the exploring era (08-19 to 09-16)</h2>
118	<h3>The 2012 tree builds and runs again</h3>
119	<ul>
120	<li>08-18 and 08-19: the tree built on JDK 8, and the team's tests ran: about 1.4K in <code>testFast</code>, 382 in <code>testSystem</code>.</li>
121	<li>08-19: the first toy programs ran under walk: a small demo, a Mandelbrot set ported from Swift and rewritten in plain Fortress style, and a ring of complex numbers.</li>
122	<li>To 08-21: the build moved to current Java and Scala. These 21 commits change nothing a Fortress programmer sees. The suites ran in parallel, 13 minutes down to 7.5.</li>
123	</ul>
124	<p class="lead"><strong>The microGPT rounds (08-23 to 08-30).</strong> microGPT is Karpathy's small GPT in Python. Each round wrote it in Fortress and ran it under walk. A golden check compared its numbers with the Python program's.</p>
125	<ul>
126	<li>Round 1 (08-23): a direct port. A training step took 10.9 s, against 22 ms in Python.</li>
127	<li>08-24 to 08-30: rewrites in plain Fortress style and in a paper's mathematical style, then a second iteration. The session began to use worker agents.</li>
128	</ul>
129	<p class="lead"><strong>Other assistants (09-07 to 09-11).</strong> Astra, another company's coding assistant, wrote its own port outside this session, with no transcript. Later Astra and a second assistant, Sol, joined a discussion of notation.</p>
130	<h3>Blinded and informed runs (09-08 to 09-15)</h3>
131	<ul>
132	<li>The blinded run (09-08): a fresh session that could not read the earlier ports. It worked alone for 1.5 hours and wrote 15 gap rows.</li>
133	<li>Run B (09-09) could read the earlier work: 25 gap rows. Run B2, the coordinating session's own attempt: 39 rows.</li>
134	<li>Runs C to C4 (09-14) used a "flat" layout, after Hsu's data design for array languages. C4 merged C2 and C3: a 71-line model passing 40 of 40 checks.</li>
135	<li>The APL side quest (to 09-15): APL notation turned into Fortress code by a grammar extension, then microGPT written in it. The curator closed the quest there.</li>
136	</ul>
137	<p class="lead"><strong>The ledger's first 309 rows.</strong> Each run wrote a gap table. A merge worker re-ran every reproducer, numbered the rows and folded repeats: 83 rows on 09-08, 139 after Runs B and B2, 265 after the APL merge, rows 288 to 310 on 09-16. Row 148 is empty on purpose. <code>FACTS.md</code> (what is established) and <code>POSITIONS.md</code> (the curator's decisions) began on 09-15.</p>
138	<p class="lead"><strong>The territory map (09-16).</strong> Before any edit to the team's code, five surveys described the system: modules, what of the specification is built, tests, dormant code, and the designers' reasons. Two of its facts: the interpreter's tests and the compiler's tests share no program; <code>Specification-1.0-frozen/</code> is the team's draft of 2011-02-02, and <code>Specification/</code> is the text the revival revises.</p>
139	<p class="lead"><strong>The sealed tree.</strong> The tag <code>sealed-tree</code> (<code>75cca6683</code>, 09-16) is the last commit before the revival edited the team's code.</p>
140	<p class="sources"><span class="label">Sources</span> <code>explorations/process-records/01-microgpt-port.md</code> to <code>14-apl-rung-4b.md</code>; <code>explorations/microgpt-run-c-handover.md</code>; <code>explorations/microgpt-run-c-handover-history.md</code>; <code>explorations/coordinator/map/README.md</code>; <code>explorations/coordinator/process-engineering/gap-ledger-archaeology.md</code> section 1.1; <code>explorations/coordinator/process-engineering/fortress-changes-chronology.md</code> A0, A1, A10; <code>explorations/notation-collaboration/README.md</code>.</p>
141	</section>
142	<section id="part2" class="part">
143	<h2><span class="num">2</span>The unsealed tree and the batches (09-17 to 10-03)</h2>
144	<p class="lead"><strong>How a batch works.</strong> A batch is one run of a workflow script.</p>
145	<ul>
146	<li>A <strong>rung</strong> is one piece of work, done by a worker in its own checkout of the tree.</li>
147	<li>A <strong>skeptic</strong> checks each rung. If it refuses, a <strong>judge</strong> rules, a <strong>repair</strong> agent fixes, and a second skeptic checks.</li>
148	<li>The <strong>gather</strong> folds each rung's notes into the record. A <strong>merged-diff review</strong> reads the combined change.</li>
149	<li>The <strong>gate</strong> is the full check before landing: the build, both suites, 13 compiled <code>atomic</code> programs at four threads, the ladder (below), and the checker's error counts. To <strong>land</strong> is to commit to <code>main</code> and push.</li>
150	<li>After each batch, one reviewer checks it against the decisions and the designers' intent.</li>
151	</ul>
152	<p class="lead"><strong>The first climbs (09-17 to 09-20).</strong> The ladder is 410 of the team's test programs pushed through the compiled path. On the sealed tree 59 passed.</p>
153	<ul>
154	<li>The ladder climb (09-17): eight rungs added names to the prelude and compiled a top-level <code>var</code>. 81 passed. A review found one rung against the specification and one departing from it without a reason.</li>
155	<li>The repair batch (09-18 to 09-19) fixed those two.</li>
156	<li>Batches 1 and 2 (09-19, 09-20) added more prelude names: <code>RR64</code>'s functions, <code>Maybe</code>, <code>GCD</code>, shifts, integer conversions. 85 passed, and 85 still pass.</li>
157	</ul>
158	<p class="lead"><strong>Batches 3 to 10 (09-22 to 10-03).</strong> From 09-21 every batch worked to make the checker accept the one library: 16 landings in 12 days. Names such as 3.5, 6b, 7R, N or 7b are batches split from or added to a plan. Batches 8 to 10 repaired checker defects and the library's one-off slips. The decisions below say which batch built each one.</p>
159	<h3>The main decisions, in plain words</h3>
160	<ul>
161	<li><strong>The goal</strong> (09-16). Finish what the designers intended, judged by the specification as revised. The measure is microGPT compiled and running fast. The tree was unsealed on 09-17.</li>
162	<li><strong>Test first</strong> (09-17). Every edit starts from a failing test that stays in the suite. Expected-failure tests (files named <code>XXX…</code>) grew from 56 to 229.</li>
163	<li><strong>One library</strong> (09-21). The interpreter's library becomes the only one. The prelude gets no new declaration and goes at the switch-over. The rungs that added prelude names stopped, and the checker's errors on the one library became the measure.</li>
164	<li><strong>The flat number tower</strong> (09-24). The checker keeps the team's rule that no type extends two instantiations of one generic trait. The one library's nested number types broke it, so they became siblings that convert by declared coercions, as in the prelude. Walk learned coercion in batch 4; the library was flattened in batch 6. The checker's errors fell from 125 to 62.</li>
165	<li><strong>Sizes at run time</strong> (09-24). A size parameter (<code>nat</code>) gets the run-time descriptor a type argument has. Since batch 5, a sized program compiles and runs.</li>
166	<li><strong>Overflow</strong> (09-26). Fixed-width overflow raises <code>IntegerOverflow</code> under walk too. Code meant to wrap uses the specification's operators ∔ ∸ ⨰. Batches 5 and 6b.</li>
167	<li><strong>The plan</strong> (09-26). Six phases: 1 and 2, decided work and the flattening; 3, the checker at zero errors; 4, the switch-over; 5, microGPT compiles; 6, microGPT fast. Phase 3 paused after batch 10; phases 4 to 6 have not started.</li>
168	<li><strong>Ranges over <code>ZZ32</code> only</strong>, because a JVM array index is a 32-bit integer. Batch 7R.</li>
169	<li><strong>The inference rule.</strong> When a generic call mixes number types, the type parameter takes the narrowest type all arguments convert into. A parameter nothing fixes takes its declared bound, never the empty type <code>Bottom</code>. Batches N, 8 and 9.</li>
170	<li><strong>The overloading model.</strong> Overloads may differ in static parameters. The return-type rule is checked for every instance. Walk chooses by declared types, not by order in the file. Batch 7b.</li>
171	<li><strong><code>fill</code> takes a value, <code>tabulate</code> a function.</strong> Batch 7.</li>
172	<li><strong>Arrays become unboxed <code>double[]</code></strong> (09-19). Decided, not built.</li>
173	<li><strong>Each change to the specification</strong> gets a note at the passage and an Appendix I entry with the original text and the reason.</li>
174	</ul>
175	<p class="lead"><strong>The checker's errors falling.</strong> Two numbers measure the way to the switch-over.</p>
176	<ul>
177	<li><strong>The checker count</strong>: the errors the checker reports on the one library. The checker stops early in each api file, so the count misses errors behind the stop. A gate stage from batch 3.</li>
178	<li><strong>The distance to the switch-over</strong>: every error over all the library's files, with no early stop. First measured before batch 7.</li>
179	<li>Neither makes the gate red. A fix can raise both, when it lets the checker reach code it could not reach before.</li>
180	</ul>
181	<p class="figures-title">The checker count, where it moved</p>
182	<ul class="figures">
183	<li><span class="when">before batch 3</span><span class="val">93</span><span class="note"></span></li>
184	<li><span class="when">after 3</span><span class="val">103</span><span class="note"></span></li>
185	<li><span class="when">after 4</span><span class="val">125</span><span class="note">a fixed crash showed 22 hidden errors</span></li>
186	<li><span class="when">after 6</span><span class="val">62</span><span class="note">the flat number tower</span></li>
187	<li><span class="when">after 7</span><span class="val">22</span><span class="note"></span></li>
188	<li><span class="when">after 7R</span><span class="val">10</span><span class="note"></span></li>
189	<li><span class="when">after 7C</span><span class="val">75</span><span class="note">the checker now reads a clause it used to stop at</span></li>
190	<li><span class="when">after 7b</span><span class="val">59</span><span class="note"></span></li>
191	<li><span class="when">after 8</span><span class="val">56</span><span class="note"></span></li>
192	<li><span class="when">after 9 and 10</span><span class="val">1</span><span class="note">a pair in <code>LexicographicReduction</code> (row 582), left by the curator's choice</span></li>
193	</ul>
194	<p class="figures-title">The distance to the switch-over, where it moved</p>
195	<ul class="figures">
196	<li><span class="when">before batch 7</span><span class="val">1.7K</span><span class="note">first measured</span></li>
197	<li><span class="when">after 7</span><span class="val">940</span><span class="note"></span></li>
198	<li><span class="when">after 7R</span><span class="val">627</span><span class="note"></span></li>
199	<li><span class="when">after 7b</span><span class="val">598</span><span class="note"></span></li>
200	<li><span class="when">after 8</span><span class="val">565</span><span class="note"></span></li>
201	<li><span class="when">after 9</span><span class="val">340</span><span class="note"></span></li>
202	<li><span class="when">after 10</span><span class="val">253</span><span class="note"></span></li>
203	</ul>
204	<p>Of the 253: about 95 are arrays, waiting for the array design; 36 are ranges held for the curator; 30 are in bodies of self-typed traits and wait for a judgement.</p>
205	<p class="lead"><strong>Where the tokens went.</strong> "Written" means tokens processed fresh: cache writes and new input, the measure the curator uses.</p>
206	<ul>
207	<li>The 15 batches from the repair batch to 6.5b used 269 agents and wrote 146M tokens.</li>
208	<li>Rung workers took about half; skeptics, judges, repairs and reviews about a third.</li>
209	<li>45% of all writes came after an agent waited five minutes or more. The prompt cache lives five minutes, so the agent wrote its whole context again.</li>
210	<li>The largest batches: 7 (22M), N (18M), 6.5b (17M). After the post-mortem: 7b 7.3M, 8 and 9 14.6M together, 10 6.3M.</li>
211	</ul>
212	<h3>Where the records went: the post-mortem of 09-29</h3>
213	<ul>
214	<li>Batch 6.5b changed about 280 lines of Fortress code, 90 of specification and 710 of tests. Beside them it committed 866 record files, 46K lines, most of them scratch.</li>
215	<li>Nobody had asked the curator whether scratch goes to <code>main</code>. It came from six small steps. On 09-19 he asked whether logs were needed. Gate logs stayed out, but rung outputs were then named <code>.txt</code>, so <code>.gitignore</code> missed them, and he was not told.</li>
216	<li>Two cleanings removed 4.4K files (11.7 MB) on 09-29 and 9.9K files (173 MB) on 09-30.</li>
217	<li>The new practice: commit only what <code>main</code> is to hold; commit the test alone and see it fail first; build or run nothing twice. Batch 7b committed 20 record files.</li>
218	</ul>
219	<p class="sources"><span class="label">Sources</span> <code>explorations/microgpt-run-c-handover.md</code> and <code>explorations/microgpt-run-c-handover-history.md</code> (the landing paragraphs); <code>explorations/coordinator/PLAN.md</code> ("The phases"); <code>explorations/coordinator/POSITIONS.md</code>; <code>explorations/coordinator/postmortem-2026-09-29/README.md</code> and <code>characterization.md</code>; <code>explorations/reviews/batch-6.5-review.md</code> to <code>batch-10-review.md</code> (first sections); <code>explorations/compile-ladder/CLIMB.md</code>; <code>explorations/coordinator/process-engineering/README.md</code>.</p>
220	</section>
221	<section id="part3" class="part">
222	<h2><span class="num">3</span>How the record grew</h2>
223	<figure><img src="record-growth.png" alt="The gap ledger&#x27;s characters and rows, and FACTS, POSITIONS and INDEX, commit by commit from 09-08 to 10-09, with each batch&#x27;s landing marked" width="1080" height="1620"><figcaption>The record, commit by commit, 09-08 to 10-09. Top: the gap ledger's characters. Middle: its rows. Bottom: FACTS, POSITIONS and INDEX. Each vertical line is a batch landing.</figcaption></figure>
224	<p>The chart has three panels on one time axis. A vertical line marks each batch's landing. Top: the ledger's characters. Middle: its rows. Bottom: FACTS, POSITIONS and INDEX (INDEX has one line for each note under <code>explorations/</code>).</p>
225	<ul>
226	<li>The ledger reached <code>main</code> on 09-09 with 83 rows. It reached 638 rows and 1.25M characters at batch 10 (10-03). It has 694 rows and 581K characters now.</li>
227	<li>The exploring era wrote 309 rows, 0.8K characters each on average. Today they hold 247K characters.</li>
228	<li>The batches wrote 329 rows to batch 10. At entry they were two to three times as long: 2.6K on average in the first batches, 1.4K in batches 8 to 10. Later edits made them 40% to 55% longer, to 0.88M characters on 10-03. Today they hold 275K. The 56 rows since hold 49K, 0.9K each on average.</li>
229	<li>So most of the ledger's text still comes from the batches' rows, not from the exploring era's: 275K against 247K.</li>
230	<li>Rows almost never shrank until 10-08: after entry they gained 308K characters and lost 0.8K. Then the ledger was rewritten. Each row was cut to 1,200 characters at most, and the ledger fell from 1.25M to 541K characters in one commit. The mean row went from 1.9K to 0.8K. The earlier text is in a history file, 1.33M characters now. Closing a row cuts it too.</li>
231	<li>From 09-17 to batch 13, batch runs took 28% of the clock (35% to batch 10; no batch ran from 10-03 to 10-08). They brought 89% of the ledger's new characters and 93% of its new rows (88% and 92% to batch 10).</li>
232	<li>FACTS peaked at 352K at batch 7b (09-30). On 10-02 it lost 92K in one commit, when the rung entries were cut to the fact. It is 294K now.</li>
233	<li>POSITIONS peaked at 105K (09-29) and is 94K now. INDEX has only grown, to 175K.</li>
234	</ul>
