269	<section id="part5" class="part">
270	<h2><span class="num">5</span>What the revival changed in Fortress itself</h2>
271	<p>Changes a programmer writing Fortress would notice, by area.</p>
272	<h3>Numbers</h3>
273	<ul>
274	<li>The number types are siblings under <code>Number</code>, not a tower, as the team's compiler library already had them. A wider type converts from a narrower one by a declared <code>coerce</code>.</li>
275	<li>Fixed-width overflow raises <code>IntegerOverflow</code> under walk, as on the compiled path. To wrap on purpose, write ∔, ∸ or ⨰.</li>
276	<li><code>SUM</code> and <code>PROD</code> are generic. A clause form whose element type nothing fixes must name it: <code>SUM[\ZZ32\][j &lt;- 0#i] f(j)</code>.</li>
277	<li><code>QQ</code> is exact. A negative shift count shifts the other way. <code>GCD</code> and <code>LCM</code> are never negative. <code>narrow</code> keeps the low 32 bits. <code>round</code> sends an exact half to the even integer.</li>
278	<li>The library's numeral type is <code>IntLiteral</code>, a sibling every number type converts from. Walk's numerals are unchanged for now.</li>
279	</ul>
280	<p class="lead"><strong>Ranges.</strong> Ranges are over <code>ZZ32</code> only. A range over <code>ZZ64</code>, <code>NN32</code>, <code>NN64</code> or <code>ZZ</code> stops when built.</p>
281	<h3>Arrays and the rest of the library</h3>
282	<ul>
283	<li><code>fill</code> takes a value; <code>tabulate</code> takes a function.</li>
284	<li><code>String</code>'s juxtaposition is three top-level operators.</li>
285	<li>Array storage is still boxed.</li>
286	</ul>
287	<h3>Walk</h3>
288	<ul>
289	<li>Walk converts by coercion at calls, bindings and assignments, picking the coercion from the run-time value.</li>
290	<li>Walk infers type arguments through coercions. A type parameter nothing fixes takes its bound.</li>
291	<li>Walk chooses between a generic and a plain declaration by declared types, not by order in the file.</li>
292	<li>At load, walk refuses some programs it used to run: an object extending two closed traits without being listed, and two functional methods with no declaration on their meet.</li>
293	</ul>
294	<h3>The checker</h3>
295	<ul>
296	<li>It checks <code>nat</code> and <code>int</code> size parameters, and infers static arguments through coercions.</li>
297	<li>Overloads may differ in static parameters. The return-type rule is checked for every instance.</li>
298	<li>It judges two functional methods for each type that provides both.</li>
299	</ul>
300	<h3>The code generator and the run time</h3>
301	<ul>
302	<li>A top-level <code>var</code> compiles, and <code>atomic</code> covers it.</li>
303	<li>A numeral of exactly 32 or 64 bits keeps its value.</li>
304	<li>A sized program compiles, loads and runs. Generic code dispatches correctly on four threads.</li>
305	<li>An object with no <code>asString</code> prints its type name.</li>
306	</ul>
307	<p class="lead"><strong>The specification.</strong> Appendix I has 37 entries, each with the original text and the reason.</p>
308	<h3>Size of the internal changes</h3>
309	<ul>
310	<li>122 commits touched the code, library or specification: 28 before the tag, 21 of them toolchain; 94 after it.</li>
311	<li>Lines added: walk 2.8K, checker 1.2K, code generator and run time 1.0K, specification 4.7K, tests 12K. The library gained 3.0K lines and lost 2.1K.</li>
312	<li>The grammar is unchanged. 43 of the team's test and demo files changed. The suites grew from about 1.4K and 382 tests to 1.8K and 516.</li>
313	</ul>
314	<p class="sources"><span class="label">Sources</span> <code>explorations/coordinator/process-engineering/fortress-changes-chronology.md</code> part A.</p>
315	</section>
316	<section id="part6" class="part">
317	<h2><span class="num">6</span>The original Fortress and the revival's resolutions</h2>
318	<p>The skill has a section "Fortress as a language". It has eleven points. Each point corrects a belief that an agent may bring from its training. This part says, for each point, whether the revival left the original language unchanged or resolved a contradiction in it.</p>
319	<h3>The eleven points</h3>
320	<p>These are the bullets of the section "Fortress as a language" in the <code>fortress-repo</code> skill's <code>SKILL.md</code>, numbered in their order there:</p>
321	<ol><li>Only the compiled path checks types.</li><li>A call chooses among overloaded declarations by the run-time types of all its arguments.</li><li>Functional methods: a method with <code>self</code> among its parameters is called as <code>f(x)</code>.</li><li>Traits and objects, no classes.</li><li>Static parameters, written <code>[\T\]</code>.</li><li>Numbers are side by side, not a tower.</li><li>Evaluation is parallel by default.</li><li>Loops and reductions are library code.</li><li>Juxtaposition is an operator, and spaces count.</li><li>Declarations: <code>x = e</code>, <code>var x: T = e</code>, <code>x := e</code>.</li><li>What the specification describes and nobody built.</li></ol>
322	<h3>The team left three sources that disagree</h3>
323	<p>The team did not leave one consistent language. It left three sources:</p>
324	<ul>
325	<li>the specification, the text that says what Fortress is;</li>
326	<li>the interpreter (walk) and its library;</li>
327	<li>the compiler and its own, smaller library.</li>
328	</ul>
329	<p>These sources disagree in places. Where they disagree, we chose one side. Usually we chose the side that works with the type checker.</p>
330	<h3>Example: the number types</h3>
331	<ul>
332	<li>The team's interpreter library put the number types in a tower. A narrower type was a kind of the wider type: <code>ZZ32</code> was a kind of <code>ZZ64</code>, and <code>ZZ64</code> was a kind of <code>ZZ</code>.</li>
333	<li>The team's compiler library put the number types side by side, by 2009. No number type is a kind of another. A wider type converts a narrower value when a call needs it.</li>
334	<li>In 2010 the team added a rule to the type checker: a type may not be two versions of one generic trait. In the tower, <code>ZZ32</code> is both an <code>AdditiveGroup</code> of <code>ZZ32</code> and an <code>AdditiveGroup</code> of <code>ZZ64</code>. The rule refuses this. The side-by-side types obey the rule.</li>
335	<li>On 09-24 we put the interpreter's library side by side too, so that the checker accepts it.</li>
336	</ul>
337	<p>So a model that remembers the tower remembers the team's interpreter library correctly. What it does not know is that the team's compiler had already left the tower.</p>
338	<h3>Points unchanged from the original</h3>
339	<p>These points describe the original language, unchanged:</p>
340	<ul>
341	<li>Point 3: functional methods.</li>
342	<li>Point 9: juxtaposition and spaces.</li>
343	<li>Point 10: declarations.</li>
344	<li>Point 11: what the specification describes that the team never built.</li>
345	<li>Point 8: loops and reductions are library code. A <code>for</code> loop, a comprehension and <code>SUM</code> are not built into the language. The compiler turns each one into calls to library objects: a generator, which produces the values, and a reduction, which combines them.</li>
346	</ul>
347	<p>These points are the original's, with a small revival change:</p>
348	<ul>
349	<li>Point 1: only the compiled path checks types. The team's tool turns the checker on for <code>compile</code> and off for walk. We did not change this. Walk now checks some things when it loads a program, but these are not static types.</li>
350	<li>Point 7: evaluation is parallel by default. The revival changed one thing: the test suites run on one thread, set in the build file. This is held for your review.</li>
351	
352	</ul>
353	<h3>Points where the revival resolved a contradiction</h3>
354	<p>The revival changes the original language only for one of two reasons: two of the original sources contradict each other, or the language as it was left fails on a program. Each time, the revival follows the original's latest word that works: the compiler's library, the original team's later papers, or the specification. Every change to the specification keeps the original text and the reason, in its Appendix I.</p>
355	<p>Each item below gives the original contradiction, then the revival's resolution and why the revival chose it.</p>
356	<h4>Point 2: choosing among overloaded declarations</h4>
357	<ul>
358	<li><strong>Overloads that differ in type parameters.</strong>
359	<ul>
360	<li>Original: one sentence of the specification, from 2009, forbids this. Neither implementation ever enforced it. The type group's later model of 2011 allows it, and the checker runs that model.
361	</li>
362	<li>Resolution: the revival allows such overloads, and the specification now states the 2011 model. The revival follows the type group because its model is the one that was built and used.
363	</li>
364	</ul></li>
365	<li><strong>The return-type rule.</strong>
366	<ul>
367	<li>Original: the type group's paper asks the checker to test the rule for every instance of a generic declaration. The checker tested one. A counterexample from the paper then passed the checker and failed the JVM's check of the bytecode.
368	</li>
369	<li>Resolution: the checker now tests the rule for every instance, as the paper asks. The counterexample is now refused, so the JVM no longer fails on it.
370	</li>
371	</ul></li>
372	<li><strong>How walk chooses between a generic and a plain declaration.</strong>
373	<ul>
374	<li>Original: the specification chooses by the declarations' types. Walk chose by their order in the file.
375	</li>
376	<li>Resolution: walk now chooses by the declared types, as the specification says. The answer of a program no longer depends on the order of its declarations.
377	</li></ul>
378	</li></ul>
379	<h4>Point 4: traits</h4>
380	<ul>
381	<li><strong>The rule against two versions of one generic trait.</strong>
382	<ul>
383	<li>Original: the type group's papers and the checker have the rule. The 2011 specification does not state it, and some of its examples break it.
384	</li>
385	<li>Resolution: the specification now states the rule. It keeps the examples that break it and marks them "Not allowed". The revival made this choice because the checker enforces the rule and the type group's later Types chapter of 2012 states it.
386	</li>
387	</ul></li>
388	<li><strong>What a <code>comprises</code> clause says.</strong>
389	<ul>
390	<li>Original: four older passages of the specification read it as a statement about types. They fail for programs that the specification allows. The later Types chapter reads it as a statement about values.
391	</li>
392	<li>Resolution: the revival reads it about values: every value of the trait is a value of a listed type. It follows the later Types chapter, because that reading works for the programs that the specification allows.
393	</li></ul>
394	</li></ul>
395	<h4>Point 5: static parameters</h4>
396	<ul>
397	<li><strong>The bound of a type parameter with no written bound.</strong>
398	<ul>
399	<li>Original: the 2011 specification says <code>Object</code>. The library uses its generic containers with tuples, which <code>Object</code> does not hold, and it writes <code>Any</code> where it states a bound.
400	</li>
401	<li>Resolution: the bound is <code>Any</code>. The revival chose it because the original library works only with <code>Any</code>.
402	</li>
403	</ul></li>
404	<li><strong>A type parameter that nothing in a call fixes.</strong>
405	<ul>
406	<li>Original: the implementations gave it the empty type <code>Bottom</code>, and the compiled program crashed at load. The type group's last paper, of 2019, gives it its bound.
407	</li>
408	<li>Resolution: such a parameter now takes its bound, as the 2019 paper says. The compiled program no longer crashes at load for this reason.
409	</li>
410	</ul></li>
411	<li><strong>Sizes on the compiled path.</strong>
412	<ul>
413	<li>Original: the specification has size parameters. The compiled path could not carry them, but its code generator had a slot reserved for them.
414	</li>
415	<li>Resolution: the revival fills the reserved slot, in the original pattern for type arguments. This completes the original design, and a program with sizes now compiles and runs.
416	</li></ul>
417	</li></ul>
418	<h4>Point 6: numbers</h4>
419	<ul>
420	<li><strong>Tower or side by side.</strong>
421	<ul>
422	<li>Original: the interpreter's library had a tower: <code>ZZ32</code> was a kind of <code>ZZ64</code>. The compiler's library had the number types side by side. The checker's rule against two versions of one generic trait, from 2010, refuses the tower.
423	</li>
424	<li>Resolution: the one library now has the number types side by side, as the compiler's library had them. A wider type converts from a narrower one. The revival chose this shape because the checker's rule refuses the tower.
425	</li>
426	</ul></li>
427	<li><strong>Conversion in walk.</strong>
428	<ul>
429	<li>Original: the specification says that a call converts its arguments when that makes it apply. Walk had a note "TODO add checks for COERCE" at that place.
430	</li>
431	<li>Resolution: walk now converts the arguments, as the specification says. The revival did the work that the team's note left to do.
432	</li>
433	</ul></li>
434	<li><strong>Overflow.</strong>
435	<ul>
436	<li>Original: the specification and the compiled path raise <code>IntegerOverflow</code> when a fixed-width result does not fit. Walk wrapped the result.
437	</li>
438	<li>Resolution: walk now raises <code>IntegerOverflow</code>, as the specification says and the compiled path does. Code that means to wrap uses the specification's own wrapping operators.
439	</li>
440	</ul></li>
441	<li><strong>Ranges.</strong>
442	<ul>
443	<li>Original: the interpreter's ranges took any integer type. The compiler's library had ranges over <code>ZZ32</code> only.
444	</li>
445	<li>Resolution: ranges are over <code>ZZ32</code> only, as in the compiler's library. A range counts the indices of an array, and a JVM array index is a 32-bit integer. A loop that needs a wider counter widens a <code>ZZ32</code> index.
446	</li>
447	</ul></li>
448	<li><strong><code>SUM</code> and <code>PROD</code>.</strong>
449	<ul>
450	<li>Original: <code>SUM</code> used catch-all operators on <code>Number</code>. Those operators went with the tower.
451	</li>
452	<li>Resolution: <code>SUM</code> and <code>PROD</code> are now one generic declaration each. Each uses the element type's own operator, and it takes its zero or one by a device that the library already uses for arrays. When nothing in a clause fixes the element type, the program must write it: <code>SUM[\ZZ32\][j &lt;- 0#n] f(j)</code>. This change follows from the number types side by side.
453	</li></ul>
454	</li></ul>
455	<h3>Where the quiz was right about the original</h3>
456	<p>A fresh model answered a quiz on Fortress from its training alone. The audit marked some answers as wrong. Some of these answers are right about the team's Fortress:</p>
457	<ul>
458	<li>The tower of number types: right for the team's interpreter library.</li>
459	<li>Arithmetic that wraps: right for the team's walk. The team's specification and compiler raised an error.</li>
460	<li>No conversion in walk: right for the team's walk.</li>
461	</ul>
462	<p>Other answers marked wrong come from the team's own specification. The specification describes more than the team built:</p>
463	<ul>
464	<li><code>Monoid[\T, ⊕\]</code> with laws: a chapter of the specification. The library has it only in a comment.</li>
465	<li><code>fortress run Foo.fss</code>: the specification's overview says this. The team's tool runs a program under walk with <code>fortress Foo.fss</code>.</li>
466	<li>Complex numbers: named in the specification, in no library.</li>
467	<li>Tests that state their expected output: the team's compiler tests do this. Its interpreter tests do not.</li>
468	</ul>
469	<p>Two answers are wrong for both the team and us: integer types that wrap by themselves (the specification wraps only with its special operators), and an <code>Any</code> type that does not hold tuples.</p>
470	<p class="sources"><span class="label">Sources</span> <code>explorations/coordinator/process-engineering/fortress-changes-chronology.md</code> part B; <code>.claude/skills/fortress-repo/SKILL.md</code> ("Fortress as a language"); <code>explorations/reviews/fortress-pretraining-quiz.md</code>; <code>explorations/reviews/skills-distillation-audit.md</code> section B.</p>
471	</section>
