114	<section id="part1" class="part">
115	<h2><span class="num">1</span>How the revival treats the team's work</h2>
116	<p class="lead"><strong>Why now.</strong> The team's work stopped in 2012, when Oracle Labs concluded the project. Its last commit is Karl Naden's write-up on instantiating generics at run time, on 2012-08-31. The curator took the work up in 2026. His reason, in his words: "we're trying to finish what they didn't get time and resources to do". He sees it as possible now, with frontier agentic coding systems. The project is his hobby, done with Claude in his spare time: "a personal learning exercise equal parts about Fortress as a language and agentic coding". Its main goal is a working compiler, so that one program, microGPT, a small GPT model written in Fortress, runs compiled.</p>
117	<h3>Custodians, not authors</h3>
118	<p>The first principle of the protocol the work follows reads: "We are custodians of their language, not its authors." It asks the revival to finish what the designers intended, judged by the specification and by the library's own practice. Every agent is bound by the same rules through the skill it loads.</p>
119	<ul>
120	<li>No committed file claims credit for the revival.</li>
121	<li>Where git doesn't record who wrote something, the authorship is rebuilt from the history. It's never guessed.</li>
122	<li>Reasons go into commit messages and reports, not into the team's source comments.</li>
123	<li>Every claim is checked against a primary source: the code, the specification, the papers or a command's output.</li>
124	</ul>
125	<h3>The history, kept whole</h3>
126	<ul>
127	<li>The repository keeps the team's surviving history: 5.4K commits on the trunk, from 2007-01-04 to 2012-08-31. With the old repository's twelve other branches, it's about 5.6K.</li>
128	<li>It's a 2018 fork of a git conversion of the project's java.net Mercurial repository. The conversion cut 146 links between commits, so <code>git blame</code> here credits whole files to snapshots of 2011 and 2012. Authorship is counted over the full history instead.</li>
129	<li>From that history, a map of who wrote what was rebuilt, with the evidence for each identity. The README sums it up: Sukyoung Ryu on the front end, from the parser to the type checker; David Chase on the compiler and the run time; Jan-Willem Maessen on the library; Christine Flood on transactions and the work-stealing run time; and a wider circle of students, interns and visitors.</li>
130	<li>The map also says what git can't show. The project began in 2003, four years before the record starts. So Eric Allen, a co-principal investigator from the start, shows only about 230 commits. Two co-authors of the 1.0 specification, Sam Tobin-Hochstadt and Joe Hallett, show none.</li>
131	<li>The team's frozen copy of the specification, which Guy Steele made on 2012-01-04, is untouched. It's the Working Draft as of 2011-02-02.</li>
132	</ul>
133	<h3>Where the editing began</h3>
134	<ul>
135	<li>The tag <code>sealed-tree</code> marks the territory map's last commit, of 09-16. After it, the revival edits the team's language, library and specification. The tag's message states the rule: each edit starts "from a failing test added to the corpus, with the full suite kept green".</li>
136	<li>Before the tag, 28 commits touched the team's code, library or specification. 21 of them only moved the tree onto current Java and Scala: the build, its tools, and the JDK's own fork/join pool in place of the old copy. The other 7 correct the constant e, fix a name clash between the two libraries' <code>System</code> apis, speed up the suites and fix the specification's rendering.</li>
137	<li>Also before the tag, five surveys mapped the system, down to where the designers wrote their reasons (part 2).</li>
138	</ul>
139	<h3>Test first, judged by the team's suites</h3>
140	<ul>
141	<li>Every edit of the team's code starts from a failing test in the team's suites. The fix follows, and the test stays.</li>
142	<li>The check is the suites' own verdict, pass or fail, as the team designed them. A value that matters is asserted inside the test, with the suite's own means: an <code>assert</code> in an interpreter test, a <code>run_out_equals</code> key in a compiled one. No expected-output file is added. The interpreter's suite never compared one.</li>
143	<li>A defect that's measured and not yet fixed gets an expected-failure test, named <code>XXX…</code> by the team's own convention for a test that must fail. The team left 56 in the interpreter's suite. There are 120 now.</li>
144	<li>Every landing now runs the team's two suites and the specification's 130 extracted examples, with no failure. None of the team's tests or demos has been deleted. 43 of them were edited, each for a reason on record (part 9).</li>
145	</ul>
146	<h3>Changing the team's text</h3>
147	<ul>
148	<li>The team's own sources disagree in places: the specification, the interpreter, the compiler and the type papers. The revival reconciles them. Where the type group's later work departs from the original specification, the revival follows that work: the exclusion rule, built into the checker in 2010, and the OOPSLA 2011 paper on type checking multiple dispatch. After the project came the POPL 2019 paper of Park, Hong, Steele and Ryu. The specification is then updated to match what the revival implements.</li>
149	<li>Each change to the specification follows the team's own ways of marking a revision. A labelled callout sits at the changed passage, as the team's 2009 draft put its decisions in notes at the passage. An entry in the changes appendix, Appendix I, lists it, as the 1.0 release listed its changes in an appendix. Each entry quotes the original sentences with their file and line, gives the reason, and says what the alternative not taken would need: the relaxed rule of the team's 2012 patent applications. There are 39.</li>
150	<li>The library's own practice is the standard. A fix extends the library the way the library already does the same kind of thing. No fix is designed from a reading of the specification alone.</li>
151	<li>The POPL 2019 paper is read at the source. Since 10-10 it's in the repository beside its extract, as its open license allows. Gyunghee Park's talk on it is transcribed from the ACM video. The paper and the talk hold 24 worked examples. The 15 that Fortress can write today were written as programs, run on both paths, and sorted by what each can test. The other 9 need features not built, such as declared covariance.</li>
152	</ul>
153	<p class="sources"><span class="label">Sources</span> <code>explorations/protocol.md</code> (principle 1); <code>.claude/skills/fortress-repo/SKILL.md</code> ("Rules for every task"); <code>README.md</code> ("History", "Lineage and license"); <code>research/authorship.md</code> (the caveats, the identity key, who wrote what); <code>explorations/coordinator/lineage.md</code>; <code>git cat-file -p sealed-tree</code>; <code>git rev-list --count a874948ac</code>; <code>git show c02fa44d0</code>; <code>explorations/coordinator/process-engineering/fortress-changes-chronology.md</code> A0, A1, A10; <code>explorations/coordinator/POSITIONS.md</code> ("Test first, the test kept.", "The suite's verdict is the check.", "The S1 form", "The type group's late positions outweigh the early text.", "The library's own practice is the standard."); <code>explorations/coordinator/FACTS.md</code> ("The team's latest word on types and on the exclusion rule is the unfinished restart, not <code>Specification/</code>"); <code>explorations/reviews/spec-change-form.md</code> section 5; <code>explorations/reviews/popl2019-examples.md</code>; <code>research/extracts/ParkPOPL2019-talk.md</code>; <code>explorations/compile-ladder/climb-batch-13b/gate/summary.txt</code>; <code>Specification/appendices/changes.tex</code>; the curator's message of 10-12.</p>
154	</section>
