157	<h2><span class="num">2</span>The start and the exploring era (08-19 to 09-16)</h2>
158	<h3>The 2012 tree builds and runs again</h3>
159	<ul>
160	<li>08-18 and 08-19: the tree built on JDK 8, and the team's tests ran: about 1.4K in <code>testFast</code>, 382 in <code>testSystem</code>.</li>
161	<li>08-19: the first toy programs ran under walk: a small demo, a Mandelbrot set ported from Swift and rewritten in plain Fortress style, and a ring of complex numbers.</li>
162	<li>To 08-21: the build moved to current Java and Scala. These 21 commits change nothing a Fortress programmer sees. The suites ran in parallel, 13 minutes down to 7.5.</li>
163	</ul>
164	<p class="lead"><strong>The microGPT rounds (08-23 to 08-30).</strong> microGPT is Karpathy's small GPT in Python. Each round wrote it in Fortress and ran it under walk. A golden check compared its numbers with the Python program's.</p>
165	<ul>
166	<li>Round 1 (08-23): a direct port. A training step took 10.9 s, against 22 ms in Python.</li>
167	<li>08-24 to 08-30: rewrites in plain Fortress style and in a paper's mathematical style, then a second iteration. The session began to use worker agents.</li>
168	</ul>
169	<p class="lead"><strong>Other assistants (09-07 to 09-11).</strong> Astra, another company's coding assistant, wrote its own port outside this session, with no transcript. Later Astra and a second assistant, Sol, joined a discussion of notation.</p>
170	<h3>Blinded and informed runs (09-08 to 09-15)</h3>
171	<ul>
172	<li>The blinded run (09-08): a fresh session that could not read the earlier ports. It worked alone for 1.5 hours and wrote 15 gap rows.</li>
173	<li>Run B (09-09) could read the earlier work: 25 gap rows. Run B2, the coordinating session's own attempt: 39 rows.</li>
174	<li>Runs C to C4 (09-14) used a "flat" layout, after Hsu's data design for array languages. C4 merged C2 and C3: a 71-line model passing 40 of 40 checks.</li>
175	<li>The APL side quest (to 09-15): APL notation turned into Fortress code by a grammar extension, then microGPT written in it. The curator closed the quest there.</li>
176	</ul>
177	<p class="lead"><strong>The ledger's first 309 rows.</strong> Each run wrote a gap table. A merge worker re-ran every reproducer, numbered the rows and folded repeats: 83 rows on 09-08, 139 after Runs B and B2, 265 after the APL merge, rows 288 to 310 on 09-16. <code>FACTS.md</code> and <code>POSITIONS.md</code> began on 09-15.</p>
178	<p class="lead"><strong>The territory map (09-16).</strong> Before the revival edited the team's language, five surveys described the system: modules, what of the specification is built, tests, dormant code, and the designers' reasons. Two of its facts: the interpreter's tests and the compiler's tests share no program; <code>Specification-1.0-frozen/</code> is the team's Working Draft as of 2011-02-02, and <code>Specification/</code> is the text the revival revises. The map's last commit was tagged <code>sealed-tree</code> (part 1).</p>
