182	<section id="part3" class="part">
183	<h2><span class="num">3</span>The unsealed tree and batches 1 to 10 (09-17 to 10-03)</h2>
184	<p class="lead"><strong>How a batch works.</strong> A batch is one run of a workflow script.</p>
185	<ul>
186	<li>A <strong>rung</strong> is one piece of work, done by a worker in its own checkout of the tree.</li>
187	<li>A <strong>skeptic</strong> checks each rung. Up to batch 10, if it refused, a <strong>judge</strong> ruled, a <strong>repair</strong> agent fixed, and a second skeptic checked. Since batch 11 the skeptic fixes what it finds, and a judge rules only on a fix that's contested.</li>
188	<li>The <strong>gather</strong> folds each rung's notes into the record. A <strong>merged-diff review</strong> reads the combined change.</li>
189	<li>The <strong>gate</strong> is the full check before landing: the build, both suites, compiled <code>atomic</code> programs at four threads, the ladder (below), and the checker's error counts. To <strong>land</strong> is to commit to <code>main</code> and push.</li>
190	<li>After each batch, one reviewer checks it against the decisions and the designers' intent.</li>
191	</ul>
192	<p class="lead"><strong>The first climbs (09-17 to 09-20).</strong> The ladder is 410 of the team's test programs pushed through the compiled path. On the sealed tree 59 passed.</p>
193	<ul>
194	<li>The ladder climb (09-17): eight rungs added names to the prelude and compiled a top-level <code>var</code>. 81 passed. A review found one rung against the specification and one departing from it without a reason.</li>
195	<li>The repair batch (09-18 to 09-19) fixed those two.</li>
196	<li>Batches 1 and 2 (09-19, 09-20) added more prelude names: <code>RR64</code>'s functions, <code>Maybe</code>, <code>GCD</code>, shifts, integer conversions. 85 passed, and 85 still pass.</li>
197	</ul>
198	<p class="lead"><strong>Batches 3 to 10 (09-22 to 10-03).</strong> From 09-21 every batch worked to make the checker accept the one library: 16 landings in 12 days. Names such as 3.5, 6b, 7R, N or 7b are batches split from or added to a plan. Batches 8 to 10 repaired checker defects and the library's one-off slips. The decisions below say which batch built each one.</p>
199	<h3>The main decisions, in plain words</h3>
200	<ul>
201	<li><strong>The goal</strong> (09-16). Finish what the designers intended, judged by the specification as revised. The measure is microGPT compiled and running fast. The tree was unsealed on 09-17.</li>
202	<li><strong>Test first</strong> (09-17). Every edit starts from a failing test that stays in the suite (part 1).</li>
203	<li><strong>One library</strong> (09-21). The interpreter's library becomes the only one. The prelude gets no new declaration and goes at the switch-over. The rungs that added prelude names stopped, and the checker's errors on the one library became the measure.</li>
204	<li><strong>The flat number tower</strong> (09-24). The checker keeps the team's rule that no type extends two instantiations of one generic trait. The one library's nested number types broke it, so they became siblings that convert by declared coercions, as in the prelude. Walk learned coercion in batch 4; the library was flattened in batch 6. The checker's errors fell from 125 to 62.</li>
205	<li><strong>Sizes at run time</strong> (09-24). A size parameter (<code>nat</code>) gets the run-time descriptor a type argument has. Since batch 5, a sized program compiles and runs.</li>
206	<li><strong>Overflow</strong> (09-26). Fixed-width overflow raises <code>IntegerOverflow</code> under walk too. Code meant to wrap uses the specification's operators ∔ ∸ ⨰. Batches 5 and 6b.</li>
207	<li><strong>The plan</strong> (09-26). Six phases: 1 and 2, decided work and the flattening; 3, the checker at zero errors; 4, the switch-over; 5, microGPT compiles; 6, microGPT fast. Phases 1 and 2 are done. Phase 3 has landed through batch 13b (part 6). Phases 4 to 6 haven't started.</li>
208	<li><strong>Ranges over <code>ZZ32</code> only</strong>, because a JVM array index is a 32-bit integer. Batch 7R.</li>
209	<li><strong>The inference rule.</strong> When a generic call mixes number types, the type parameter takes the narrowest type all arguments convert into. A parameter that nothing in a call fixes is inferred at its bound, not at the empty type <code>Bottom</code>, on both paths, with a few cases left under walk. Batches N, 8, 9 and 11. The rule at run time is only partly built (part 9).</li>
210	<li><strong>The overloading model.</strong> Overloads may differ in static parameters. The return-type rule is checked for every instance. Walk chooses by declared types, not by order in the file. Batch 7b.</li>
211	<li><strong><code>fill</code> takes a value, <code>tabulate</code> a function.</strong> Batch 7.</li>
212	<li><strong>Arrays become unboxed <code>double[]</code></strong> (09-19). Decided, not built.</li>
213	<li><strong>Each change to the specification</strong> gets a callout at the passage and an Appendix I entry with the original text and the reason.</li>
214	</ul>
215	<p class="lead"><strong>The checker's errors falling.</strong> Two numbers measure the way to the switch-over.</p>
216	<ul>
217	<li><strong>The checker count</strong>: the errors the checker reports on the one library. The checker stops at the first error of some checks, so the count misses errors behind the stop. A gate stage from batch 3.</li>
218	<li><strong>The distance to the switch-over</strong>: every error over all the library's files, with no early stop. First measured before batch 7.</li>
219	<li>Neither makes the gate red. A fix can raise both, when it lets the checker reach code it couldn't reach before.</li>
220	</ul>
