318	<section id="part6" class="part">
319	<h2><span class="num">6</span>Batches 11 to 13b (10-08 to 10-10)</h2>
320	<p class="lead"><strong>What they were for.</strong> Phase 3 went on: make the checker accept the one library, so that the compiled path can move onto it. Batches 11, 12 and 13 landed on 10-09. Batch 13b landed on 10-10, outside a batch, at the curator's word. Each batch built on the curator's answers to the questions its record put, or on the default on record where he hadn't answered yet.</p>
321	<h3>What changed</h3>
322	<ul>
323	<li><strong>Batch 11</strong> was the first run of the redesigned workflow, on the rewritten skill. Walk leaves open a type parameter whose bound names itself, so <code>SUM[i &lt;- 1#100] i</code> runs again under walk without its element type written. Under walk, a trait's <code>override</code> now overrides for every type below it. The checker reads what a type provides as the traits chapter says, by which the team's own test <code>disp0.fss</code> is valid. The ranges of rank 2 and 3 got types for their bounded ranges.</li>
324	<li><strong>Batch 12.</strong> Walk now refuses at load what the traits chapter makes a static error: an object that leaves an inherited abstract method without a body, and an <code>override</code> that overrides nothing. A range of rank 2 or 3 is checked against an array's bounds corner by corner, so a corner outside them raises <code>IndexOutOfBounds</code>. Reductions without an identity, such as <code>BIG MAX</code>, are typed at their element type.</li>
325	<li><strong>Batch 13.</strong> The checker accepts arithmetic in a size, and a size known only at run time. For the second it restores a <code>comprises</code> clause the team had left as a comment on <code>NatParam</code>. Pairs and triples compare only elements that have an order, an answer to the team's own comment on them: "Shouldn't these operators have to extend something?" A generator has a size by default, counted by running it.</li>
326	<li><strong>Batch 13b.</strong> Batch 13 built a bound for the array family, <code>T extends { Number, MultiplicativeRing[\T\] }</code>, and held it back. Under it the checker took 17 minutes on the library's api, past the stage's limit of 15. A prototype found the cause: the checker expanded <code>Number</code>'s <code>comprises</code> clause again at every subtype question. A memo of the answers that don't depend on context cut the count's stage from 24 minutes to 6 and the distance's from 76 to 31, with every answer the same. The fix and the held bound landed together.</li>
327	</ul>
328	<h3>What they moved</h3>
329	<ul>
330	<li>The distance fell from 253 to 207, 153, 105 and, with 13b, 85.</li>
331	<li>The checker count stayed at 1, and the error behind it changed. Through batch 12 it was a pair of declarations in the library's api. Batch 13 fixed the pair, and the api now checks clean. So the checker goes on to the library's component, and its first error there stops it: <code>__bigOperator</code>'s local function <code>body(i)</code> has no type on its parameter. Once that's typed, the count will show the component's own errors, 79 by the distance, before it falls.</li>
332	<li>Every gate was green at its first run. At 13b the suites ran 1.9K and 580 tests and the specification's 130 examples with no failure, and 42 compiled <code>atomic</code> runs passed at four threads.</li>
333	<li>microGPT still runs under walk, and its quick check passes at each landing. On the compiled path the 18 files of its two versions all stop at name resolution, as expected before the switch-over.</li>
334	</ul>
335	<h3>The new workflow, measured</h3>
336	<ul>
337	<li>Batches 11, 12 and 13 wrote 5.0M, 5.4M and 7.2M tokens. Batches 8 to 10 wrote 7.7M, 6.9M and 6.3M. The mean fell 15%.</li>
338	<li>The saving is the second round's removal. Refusal cycles took 20% to 28% of a batch before, and 3% to 6% after.</li>
339	<li>Batch 13 cost the most. A VM restart stopped two skeptics, one rung timed the slow checker twice, and a third ruling was needed.</li>
340	<li>Quality held, as far as the reviews can tell. Every rung was judged in the spirit of the decisions. No wrong value landed, and no review found a blocking defect. The skeptics made 57 fixes, all sound and test first. The six rulings were all upheld.</li>
341	</ul>
342	<h3>What is left</h3>
343	<ul>
344	<li>Of the 85, 29 are bodies over the self-typed <code>Integral</code> trait, which wait for a judgement on how to type them. 19 are in the array family. 16 are one-off slips and limits of the checker. The rest are small groups.</li>
345	<li>Each batch recorded the new gaps it met on the way and listed items for the curator: 24 in batch 11, 22 in batch 12, 53 in batch 13. Of batch 13's that reached PLAN, all but one carried a default that had already landed, or were for information (part 7).</li>
346	<li>No batch 14 runs until the <code>coordinator</code> and <code>cloud-container</code> skills are rewritten (part 7).</li>
347	</ul>
