352	<h2><span class="num">7</span>Since 10-10: the study's close and the coordinator rewrite</h2>
353	<p class="lead"><strong>The process-engineering study closed</strong> on 10-10 with two reports. They ask whether the measures of 10-08 did what each was for.</p>
354	<ul>
355	<li>The workflow's redesign met its aim. A batch writes 15% fewer tokens and a rung 27% fewer, and nothing measured shows a loss of quality.</li>
356	<li>The skill met one aim and missed its price. Agents stopped relearning what it covers: calls that read a script to learn how to run it fell by 70%. But it costs 0.47M to 0.65M tokens a batch, two to three times the estimate.</li>
357	<li>The skill's main aim, a correct model of Fortress, hasn't been measured. A quiz with the skill and without it would measure it for about 0.15M.</li>
358	<li>The record's new form worked for the ledger, not yet for the rest. FACTS grows back, and PLAN held 202 entries listed for the curator.</li>
359	<li>Nothing distils the record for the curator. A batch hands him tens of items. Of the 47 that batch 13 put in PLAN, one needed his word. The rest carried a landed default or were for information. The first step proposed is one short queue of what needs him.</li>
360	<li>The same day the review queue was rewritten: 50 items that need him, 122 routine ones, and 76 already settled.</li>
361	</ul>
362	<p class="lead"><strong>The map before the rewrite</strong> (10-10). Before the coordinator's skill is rewritten, a worker mapped the coordinator's records: what they hold, who reads them, and where they repeat.</p>
363	<ul>
364	<li>A boot, after a compaction or a restart, reads eight things: about 243K tokens of record, about 317K with the session's fixed part. They grew by half in two weeks.</li>
365	<li>73 rules of conduct are stated in 249 places. Only two of them have a single home.</li>
366	<li>A fifth to a quarter of FACTS, and about a tenth of POSITIONS, is history rather than what holds now.</li>
367	</ul>
368	<p class="lead"><strong>The coordinator rewrite</strong> is under way. Its goals, as the curator corrected them on 10-10:</p>
369	<ul>
370	<li>One home for how the coordinator works. The protocol retires, and its rules that bind workers move to the workers' skill.</li>
371	<li>One home and a stated purpose for every kind of record.</li>
372	<li>A lighter boot that keeps what he relies on. The coordinator knows the project without him re-explaining it, never re-asks a decision, and never sends a worker to measure again what's on record.</li>
373	<li>No role lost: executive assistant, batch coordinator, roadmap architect, briefer of workers, analyst.</li>
374	<li>The skill rests on the model's own habits, not on hundreds of small rules. In his words: "Organization of the knowledge base should reflect the model's second nature, never fight it."</li>
375	</ul>
376	<p>Done so far: an interview of the two current models, Fable and Opus, one question per turn, as their natural baseline. Two sorts of the 73 rules against it. An archaeology of how ten duplicated rules spread. The boot note and the list of held points now have files of their own. A rule is kept when the failure it guards against is on record.</p>
377	<p>The order from here. FACTS and POSITIONS are rewritten slice by slice, each earlier text kept word for word in a history file, and each slice shown to him on a page. Then an Opus writer writes the coordinator skill from scratch, followed by a cold read and his reading. Then the <code>cloud-container</code> skill is rewritten the same way. Batch 14 comes after both.</p>
