381	<section id="part8" class="part">
382	<h2><span class="num">8</span>What the revival changed in Fortress itself</h2>
383	<p>Changes a programmer writing Fortress would notice, by area. Part 9 gives the reason for each.</p>
384	<h3>Numbers</h3>
385	<ul>
386	<li>The number types are siblings under <code>Number</code>, not a tower, as the team's prelude already had them. A wider type converts from a narrower one by a declared <code>coerce</code>.</li>
387	<li>Fixed-width overflow raises <code>IntegerOverflow</code> under walk, as on the compiled path. To wrap on purpose, write ∔, ∸ or ⨰.</li>
388	<li><code>SUM</code> and <code>PROD</code> are generic. Under walk, a reduction whose element type nothing fixes runs. The checker still needs it written: <code>SUM[\ZZ32\][j &lt;- 0#i] f(j)</code>.</li>
389	<li><code>QQ</code> is exact. A negative shift count shifts the other way. <code>GCD</code> and <code>LCM</code> are never negative. <code>narrow</code> keeps the low 32 bits. <code>round</code> sends an exact half to the even integer.</li>
390	<li><code>QQ</code>'s <code>floor</code>, <code>ceiling</code>, <code>round</code> and <code>truncate</code> throw <code>DivisionByZero</code> at +∞, −∞ and 0/0.</li>
391	<li>The library's numeral type is <code>IntLiteral</code>, a sibling that the integer types, <code>QQ</code> and <code>RR64</code> convert from. Walk's numerals are unchanged for now.</li>
392	</ul>
393	<h3>Ranges</h3>
394	<ul>
395	<li>Ranges are over <code>ZZ32</code> only. A range over <code>ZZ64</code>, <code>NN32</code>, <code>NN64</code> or <code>ZZ</code> stops when built.</li>
396	<li>Ranges of rank 2 and 3 have bounded types of their own, <code>BoundedRange2D</code> and <code>BoundedRange3D</code>. <code>#(0,n)</code> is empty.</li>
397	<li>An array subscript of rank 2 or 3 is checked corner by corner, so a corner outside the bounds raises <code>IndexOutOfBounds</code>.</li>
398	<li>The open range <code>(:)</code> can't be cut: its <code>truncL</code>, <code>truncR</code>, <code>every</code>, <code>imposeStride</code> and <code>atMost</code> fail.</li>
399	</ul>
400	<h3>Arrays and the rest of the library</h3>
401	<ul>
402	<li><code>fill</code> takes a value; <code>tabulate</code> takes a function.</li>
403	<li>The array types bound their elements: <code>T extends { Number, MultiplicativeRing[\T\] }</code>.</li>
404	<li><code>String</code>'s <code>left</code> and <code>right</code> answer <code>Just</code> of a character, or <code>Nothing</code>. <code>String</code>'s juxtaposition is three top-level operators.</li>
405	<li>Pairs and triples compare only elements that have an order. Under walk, <code>(1,2) &lt; (1,2.5)</code> is refused.</li>
406	<li>A reduction without an identity lifts to <code>Maybe[\R\]</code>.</li>
407	<li>Every generator has a size. The default counts its elements.</li>
408	<li>Array storage is still boxed.</li>
409	</ul>
410	<h3>Walk</h3>
411	<ul>
412	<li>Walk converts by coercion at calls, bindings and assignments, picking the coercion from the run-time value.</li>
413	<li>Walk infers type arguments through coercions. A type parameter nothing fixes takes its bound, and one whose bound names itself stays open.</li>
414	<li>Walk chooses between a generic and a plain declaration by declared types, not by order in the file.</li>
415	<li>A trait's <code>override</code> overrides for every type below it.</li>
416	<li>At load, walk refuses some programs it used to run: an object extending two closed traits without being listed; two functional methods with no declaration on their meet, in generic types and object expressions too; an object that leaves an inherited abstract method without a body; an <code>override</code> that overrides nothing.</li>
417	<li>Five of the team's demos leave an inherited abstract method without a body, so walk now refuses them. Two of them, <code>BirdCount1z</code> and <code>BirdCount2a</code>, ran before.</li>
418	</ul>
419	<h3>The checker</h3>
420	<ul>
421	<li>It checks <code>nat</code> and <code>int</code> size parameters, arithmetic in a size, and a size known only at run time.</li>
422	<li>It infers static arguments through coercions. A type parameter nothing fixes is inferred at its bounds, never at <code>Bottom</code>.</li>
423	<li>Overloads may differ in static parameters. The return-type rule is checked for every instance.</li>
424	<li>It judges two functional methods for each type that provides both, and reads what a type provides as the traits chapter says.</li>
425	<li>It refuses a parameter written without a type, with "Missing parameter type for".</li>
426	<li>It gives a call its expected type in more places: an <code>if</code> without <code>else</code>, a <code>typecase</code> branch, a <code>label</code>'s body, an operator repeated between operands, a tight juxtaposition.</li>
427	<li>It remembers the subtype answers that don't depend on context, so it no longer expands <code>Number</code>'s <code>comprises</code> clause again at every subtype question.</li>
428	</ul>
429	<h3>The code generator and the run time</h3>
430	<ul>
431	<li>A top-level <code>var</code> compiles, and <code>atomic</code> covers it.</li>
432	<li>A numeral of exactly 32 or 64 bits keeps its value.</li>
433	<li>A sized program compiles, loads and runs. A size computed in a type names one class: <code>Box[\2 + 1\]</code> and <code>Box[\3\]</code> are the same.</li>
434	<li>Generic code dispatches correctly on four threads.</li>
435	<li>An object with no <code>asString</code> prints its type name.</li>
436	</ul>
437	<p class="lead"><strong>The specification.</strong> Appendix I has 39 entries, each with the original text and the reason.</p>
438	<h3>Size of the internal changes</h3>
439	<ul>
440	<li>143 commits touched the code, library or specification: 28 before the tag, 21 of them toolchain; 115 after it.</li>
441	<li>Lines added after the tag: walk 3.3K, checker 1.8K, code generator and run time 1.0K, specification 5.3K, tests 14.5K. The library gained 3.6K lines and lost 2.3K.</li>
442	<li>The grammar is unchanged. The syntax tree gained one node. 43 of the team's test and demo files changed, and none was deleted. The suites grew from about 1.4K and 382 tests to 1.9K and 580.</li>
443	</ul>
