447	<section id="part9" class="part">
448	<h2><span class="num">9</span>The original Fortress and the revival's resolutions</h2>
449	<p>The skill every agent loads has a section, "Fortress as a language". Each of its points corrects a belief an agent may bring from its training. A part of the skill lists, under the same points, each place where the revival changed the team's Fortress, with the original, the resolution and the reason. This part tells it in plain words.</p>
450	<h3>The team left three sources that disagree</h3>
451	<p>The team left Fortress in three forms, with the type papers beside them:</p>
452	<ul>
453	<li>the specification, the text that says what Fortress is;</li>
454	<li>the interpreter (walk) and its library;</li>
455	<li>the compiler and its own, smaller library.</li>
456	</ul>
457	<p>These disagree in places. The revival changes the team's Fortress for one of two reasons only: two of these sources contradict each other, or a program fails. It reconciles them. Where the team's later work, in the compiler's library or the type papers, departs from the original specification, the revival follows the later work. It then updates the specification to match what it implements. A callout marks the passage, and Appendix I keeps the original text with the reason.</p>
458	<h3>Example: the number types</h3>
459	<ul>
460	<li>The team's interpreter library put the number types in a tower. A narrower type was a kind of the wider type: <code>ZZ32</code> was a kind of <code>ZZ64</code>, and <code>ZZ64</code> a kind of <code>ZZ</code>.</li>
461	<li>The prelude went from siblings to a tower and back in 2009. Christine Flood declared its widths as sibling objects in March. Jan-Willem Maessen made them a tower later that month. David Chase cut the subtyping between the widths in August. Sukyoung Ryu added the first exclusion, <code>ZZ32 excludes ZZ64</code>, in October. Maessen and Eric Allen widened the exclusions and added the first coercions, from November 2009 to January 2010. Guy Steele built the comparisons and the integer operators on the flat prelude in 2011.</li>
462	<li>In 2010 Justin Hilburn built the exclusion rule into the checker: no type may extend two instantiations of one generic trait. In the tower, <code>ZZ32</code> is both an <code>Integral</code> of <code>ZZ32</code> and, through <code>ZZ64</code>, an <code>Integral</code> of <code>ZZ64</code>, which the rule refuses. The siblings obey it.</li>
463	<li>On 09-24 the curator chose to keep the rule. Batch 6 made the interpreter's library siblings too, on 09-27, so that the checker accepts it.</li>
464	</ul>
465	<p>So a model that remembers the tower remembers the team's interpreter library correctly. What it doesn't know is that the team's compiler had left the tower in 2009.</p>
466	<h3>Left as the team had it</h3>
467	<ul>
468	<li>Functional methods: a method with <code>self</code> among its parameters is called as <code>f(x)</code>.</li>
469	<li>Juxtaposition as an operator, and the whitespace rules. The grammar is unchanged.</li>
470	<li>Declarations: <code>x = e</code>, <code>var x: T = e</code>, <code>x := e</code>.</li>
471	<li>Loops and reductions are library code. A <code>for</code> loop, a comprehension and <code>SUM</code> turn into calls to library objects: a generator, which produces the values, and a reduction, which combines them.</li>
472	<li>Evaluation is parallel by default. The revival pins the suites to four threads in the build file, so every run is parallel and alike on any machine.</li>
